<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Wassoulou Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">1878 CE – 1898 CE · capital Bissandugu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The empire of Samori Toure, which fought French expansion in West Africa for nearly two decades with modern weapons and a disciplined army.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samori-ture">Samori Toure</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Maninka</a> trader turned soldier, built a following in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> region from the 1860s, conquering small chiefdoms in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassoulou">Wassoulou</a> and Konyan areas. By 1878 he ruled a substantial state with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bissandugu">Bissandugu</a>, and in 1884 he took the Muslim title almamy.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Samori governed through provinces run by relatives and trusted commanders, with a standing army of sofa infantry. He bought repeating rifles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a>, paid for with gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves, and had local smiths copy them. He promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Qur'anic</a> schools. After clashes from 1882 and treaties in 1886 and 1887, he fought the French repeatedly, and in the 1890s moved his whole state east, to the lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kong-empire">Kong Empire</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the east he destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kong-cote-divoire">Kong</a> and fought the British and French. On 29 September 1898 the French captured him at Guelemou. Exiled to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gabon-french-colony">Gabon</a>, he died in 1900, remembered across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> as a hero of resistance.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/wassoulou-empire.jpg" alt="Wassoulou Empire" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: An Encore Performance From The Boys In The Band and HetmanTheResearcher · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Possible_Flag_of_the_Wassoulou_Empire.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1878 CE – 1898 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bissandugu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Samori Toure</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Maninka</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Guinea, Mali, Ivory Coast</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wassulu Empire, Samori's empire, Mandinka Empire</dd></dl></aside></div>

